  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069862/assets/98edb404419e7ab515109f87addb44709513007b1a0105b648a3ce2b62ad547f.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{cot}\u2061(Bx)+D,$ where $B$ and $D$ are constants. What is $B+D?$",
  "worked_solution": "The period $T$ of the function is\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nThe formula for the period $T$ of the function $y=A\\operatorname{cot}\u2061(Bx+C)+D$ is given by \n\n$$\nT=\\frac{\\pi }{B}.\n$$\n\nApplying the formula of the period, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{B}\\,\u27f9\\,B=\\frac{\\pi }{(\\frac{\\pi }{3})}=3.\n$$\n\nThe parameter $D$ represents the vertical shift of the cotangent function. We see that the function has been translated by $1$ unit up. Hence, $D=1.$\n\nTherefore, $B+D=3+1=4.$\n\nFinally, the equation of the curve is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{cot}\u2061(3x)+1.\n$$",
